YUME Debian source package notes
================================

Bundled dependency trees (basefwx/, vendor/ and top-level third_party/) are
excluded through Files-Excluded in debian/copyright. Debian builds must not
download dependencies. Use scripts/make_debian_orig.sh to create the matching
../yume_<version>.orig.tar.xz before dpkg-buildpackage -S. Upstream
0.3.0-dev1 maps to 1:0.3.0~dev1; the Debian-only epoch preserves package
ordering and is omitted from source filenames and YUME's reported version.

The Debian build selects the native YTP/1 application, leaves out the BaseFWX
modules, the GUI and the experimental SDK, and uses system JSON, nghttp2,
libsystemd and dynamically linked OpenSSL. It emits yume and yume-daemon.
The schema-1 setup/doctor scripts are installed with the client. The daemon
ships an unprovisioned schema-1 bootstrap and a disabled service. No private
keys or usable authorization records are shipped or generated on install.

The package is UNRELEASED and deliberately fails against stock Debian
libssl-dev: native TLS requires the reviewed ClientHello API in
patches/openssl/series. The build environment must provide a separately
packaged OpenSSL 3.5+ carrying that API. Publishing that dependency and naming
its exact binary-package requirement remain publication prerequisites.
debian/rules never downloads or builds OpenSSL. Dynamic linking lets
dpkg-shlibdeps record its runtime edge; debian/control also states the 3.5
floor because provider-based ML-DSA lookup has no 3.5-only ELF symbol.

The BaseFWX modules remain available for explicit development builds,
outside this package payload, and the GUI source does not build until it is
ported to the native runtime. Neither libyume1 nor libyume-dev is
emitted before ABI freeze and release qualification; debian/libyume1.symbols
is candidate review metadata only. Full Debian build, install, upgrade and
service tests still have to pass in the intended distribution environment.
